us), also known as the golden-headed langur, is a critically endangered species of langur endemic to Cát Bà Island, Vietnam. It is among the rarest primates in the world, and possibly the rarest primate in Asia, with population size estimated at less than 70 individuals.","revisionId":1371447976,"title":"Cat Ba langur","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cat_Ba_langur","wikidataId":"Q929055"}},{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"Griffons","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Griffon Vultures","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Typical Old World Vultures","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Griffon Vultures and Allies","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"vulture","source":"The Paleobiology Database"}],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Gyps","confidence":94,"rank":"genus","scientificName":"Gyps Savigny, 1809","status":"ACCEPTED","usageKey":2480382},"ottId":263138,"rank":"genus","scientificName":"Gyps","synonyms":["Pseudogyps"],"wikipedia":{"extract":"Gyps is a genus of Old World vultures that was proposed by Marie Jules César Savigny in 1809. Its members are sometimes known as griffon vultures.","revisionId":1314989541,"title":"Gyps","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gyps","wikidataId":"Q126630"}},{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"frogmouths","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Greater Frogmouths","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Australasian Frogmouths","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Australo-Papuan Frogmouths","source":"Catalogue of Life"}],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Podargus","confidence":94,"rank":"genus","scientificName":"Podargus Vieillot, 1818","status":"ACCEPTED","usageKey":2497108},"ottId":313122,"rank":"genus","scientificName":"Podargus","synonyms":["Celuro","Cyphorhina","Megapodargus","Micropodargus","Podagrus"],"wikipedia":{"extract":"Podargus is a small genus of birds in the frogmouth family, Podargidae. All members of this genus are found in Australia, with some species being found in Papua New Guinea, Indonesia, and the Solomon Islands, as well.","revisionId":1322810696,"title":"Podargus","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Podargus","wikidataId":"Q569096"}},{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"Great Tinamou","source":"The IUCN Red List of Threatened Species"}],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Tinamus major","confidence":99,"rank":"species","scientificName":"Tinamus major (J.F.Gmelin, 1789)","status":"ACCEPTED","usageKey":2477630},"ottId":402466,"rank":"species","scientificName":"Tinamus major","synonyms":[],"wikipedia":{"extract":"The great tinamou (Tinamus major) is a species of tinamou ground bird native to Central and South America. There are several subspecies, mostly differentiated by their coloration.","revisionId":1320595866,"title":"Great tinamou","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_tinamou","wikidataId":"Q852723"}},{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"Emperor Penguin","source":"The IUCN Red List of Threatened Species"}],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Aptenodytes forsteri","confidence":99,"rank":"species","scientificName":"Aptenodytes forsteri G.R.Gray, 1844","status":"ACCEPTED","usageKey":2481661},"ottId":494370,"rank":"species","scientificName":"Aptenodytes forsteri","synonyms":[],"wikipedia":{"extract":"The emperor penguin (Aptenodytes forsteri) is the tallest and heaviest of all living penguin species and is endemic to Antarctica. The male and female are similar in plumage and size, reaching 100 cm (39 in) in length and weighing from 22 to 45 kg (49 to 99 lb).","revisionId":1376834596,"title":"Emperor penguin","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emperor_penguin","wikidataId":"Q161829"}},{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"Eastern Woolly Lemur","source":"The IUCN Red List of Threatened Species"},{"language":"en","name":"Eastern Avahi","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Woolly Indris","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Gmelin's Woolly Lemur","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Gmelin’s Woolly Lemur my","source":"Catalogue of Life"}],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Avahi laniger","confidence":99,"rank":"species","scientificName":"Avahi laniger (Gmelin, 1788)","status":"ACCEPTED","usageKey":2436426},"ottId":516002,"rank":"species","scientificName":"Avahi laniger","synonyms":["Avahis laniger orientalis","Habrocebus lanatus","Indris longicaudatus","Iropocus laniger","Lemur laniger","Microrhynchus laniger"],"wikipedia":{"extract":"The eastern woolly lemur (Avahi laniger), also known as the eastern avahi or Gmelin's woolly lemur, is a species of woolly lemur native to eastern Madagascar, where it lives in the wet tropical rainforest at low elevations along the eastern coast of the island or they can also inhabit the northern tip of the island with other species. The woolly lemur name refers to their thick, tightly curled hair, whereas their generic name avahi refers to their high-pitched defensive call.","revisionId":1356978747,"title":"Eastern woolly lemur","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eastern_woolly_lemur","wikidataId":"Q307262"}},{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"Sunbeams","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Sunbeam Hummingbirds","source":"Catalogue of Life"}],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Aglaeactis","confidence":94,"rank":"genus","scientificName":"Aglaeactis Gould, 1848","status":"ACCEPTED","usageKey":2476303},"ottId":591138,"rank":"genus","scientificName":"Aglaeactis","synonyms":["Aglaiactis"],"wikipedia":{"extract":"Aglaeactis is a genus of hummingbirds in the family Trochilidae.","revisionId":1370996357,"title":"Aglaeactis","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aglaeactis","wikidataId":"Q516945"}},{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"Giraffe","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Girafte","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"West African","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"N